    "product_id": "72e065ef-eb4d-4e8d-9e24-afd62cb6857c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 101014\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n314 AM MST Thu Sep 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures will prevail through the rest of the\n  week, with Extreme Heat Warnings in effect for portions of the\n  lower deserts.\n\n- Daily chances for scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\n  will continue to be focused over the Eastern Arizona high\n  terrain. Any chances for lower deserts should be confined to\n  late Friday and again Sunday into Monday.\n\n- Cooling temperatures over the weekend into early next week\n  should eventually push readings back into the normal range by\n  around next Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nStrong high pressure is currently planted over the Southwestern\nU.S., but moisture levels have improved enough to bring some\nisolated showers and a few embedded storms over the past several\nhours. The high center is currently situated over far west-central\nArizona resulting in northeasterly mid-level flow, but west\nsouthwest lower level flow over much of Arizona. Moisture has \nincreased enough in the lower levels to push mixing ratios to \naround 10 g/kg over much of the area to upwards of 12 g/kg over \nsoutheast Arizona, but a strong lower level subsidence inversion \nis also in place. Convection was quite extensive during the \nevening hours across southeast Arizona/southwest New Mexico with \nthis complex sending a low level outflow and mid-level gravity \nwave east northeastward through the rest of Arizona. Isolated \nshowers have popped off with the passage of these features, \ntapping into elevated instability. We do not anticipate anything \nmore than very light rainfall amounts with any of these showers \nwith a focus mostly across western Maricopa into La Paz Counties.\n\nFor the rest of today, guidance is showing much more limited\nconvection than yesterday, in particular across southeast Arizona.\nThe subsidence inversion is likely to too strong, keeping chances\nbelow 10% across our CWA. Guidance does show some convection\ndeveloping south of the U.S./Mexico border during the evening, but\nit seems quite unlikely it will send anything up our way like what\nis currently happening. The peak strength of the ridge occurred\nlate yesterday and this will translate to the hottest \ntemperatures occurring today. The lower deserts of southeast\nCalifornia are expected to top 110 degrees with heat indices up \nto around 115 degrees, while the Arizona lower deserts should see\nhighs of 107-110 degrees and heat indices closer to 110 degrees\nduring the afternoon hours.\n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to weaken today into Friday,\nlikely weakening enough to lower temperatures a good 2-4 degrees\nacross south-central Arizona starting Friday and 1-3 degrees over\nthe western deserts. The Extreme Heat Warnings will continue\nthrough Friday for much of the lower deserts of southeast \nCalifornia and southwest Arizona, while expiring everywhere else\nthis evening. \n\nFriday should also bring another influx of moisture from the south\nsouthwest, pushing area low level mixing ratios more into a 11-12\ng/kg range. However, this may still not be enough to spark off \nmore widespread convection as the the subsidence inversion will \nstill be in place (albeit slightly weaker) and the steering flow \nis expected to turn out of the southwest. We are expecting better \nstorm coverage across the eastern Arizona high terrain on Friday, \nbut with a southwesterly steering flow it will be hard to get \nconvection to fire over the lower deserts. The NBM does indicate \n25-35% PoPs Friday evening/early Friday night from Phoenix \neastward, but that seems too optimistic. If storms do actually get\ngoing over the south-central Arizona lower deserts Friday \nevening, they will be within a high DCAPE environment which means \nstrong winds will be a possibility. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nForecast confidence for the weekend into next week remains quite\nlow, especially when looking at moisture levels and rain chances.\nGuidance does agree the subtropical ridge will get pushed to the\neast of our region over the weekend as a Pacific trough moves\nthrough the northern tier states, but the GEFS is stronger and\nfarther south with the trough compared to the EPS. The models also\ndiffer in the amount of moisture and rain chances during the\nperiod with the GEFS favoring better rain chances from Phoenix and\nareas east of Phoenix over the weekend, while the EPS favors\nbetter chances by Tuesday into the middle of next week. Needless\nto say, these differences do not inspire much confidence in the\navailable NBM/WPC PoPs. \n\nThe latest forecast places little if any PoPs on Saturday and \nSaturday night, which seems fairly reasonable, but then seems to \nbe under doing chances late Sunday. The increased PoPs of 20-40% \nthen seem too high for Monday before gradually lowering during the\nmiddle part of next week. Lately, guidance has had a tendency to \nplace fairly high PoPs during the 4-8 day range, just to back off \nas it gets into the short-term. It would seem everything would \nhave to come together nearly perfectly with the trough passing by \nto our north close enough, but not too close to scour out what \nmoisture we have in the low/mid levels. Additionally, if the \nsubtropical ridge becomes more dominant, then we will have to \nworry about too strong of a subsidence inversion. Given the fairly\nhigh model uncertainty, the forecast is likely to change.\n\nThe temperature forecast is also of lower confidence with a fairly\nwide spread starting Monday. At the moment, NBM forecast\ntemperatures call for readings more in the normal range by early\nnext week, but given the higher model spread we could end up\nwith readings slightly below or even slightly above normal.\nGuidance at least mostly agrees on the 'coolest' temperatures\nfalling on next Tuesday and Wednesday before trending upward\nagain during the latter half of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1000Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nA distant outflow moved through overnight carrying some lofted dust, \nresulting in temporary reductions in VIS below 10SM. Lingering \nhaze/lofted dust may cause slantwise visibility restrictions around \nsunrise but will improve through the rest of the morning. Otherwise, \nno major aviation weather concerns are anticipated through the TAF \nperiod under FEW-SCT mid and high cloud decks. Winds will follow \nfairly typical diurnal tendencies with a westerly shift likely \nbetween 17-19Z. Gusts to around 15 kts will be possible during the \nafternoon, relaxing after sunset. West winds are likely to increase \nonce again late evening into the overnight hours, delaying the \ntypical nocturnal E/SE wind switch.  \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation concerns are anticipated under periods of FEW-SCT \nmid and high cloud decks. Winds at KIPL will favor SE through the \nday and veer out of the southwest this evening. Speeds will \ngenerally remain below 10 kts, but there may be a period with gusts \nto 15-20 kts during the evening. The possibility that a gusty \noutflow from distant TS to the south may reach the terminal mid to \nlate afternoon cannot be ruled out, but is too low probability for \nTAQF inclusion. Winds at KBLH will continue to favor S to SW, with \nspeeds generally AOB 12 kts. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAbove normal temperatures will continue into the weekend with\nmodestly elevated humidities. Rain chances will improve by \nFriday, but still mostly favor the Arizona high terrain. \nAfternoon minimum humidities of 17-25% today will improve to\n25-30% for Friday and Saturday. Overnight recoveries will remain\nfair to good at 40-70%. Expect fairly typical diurnal winds with \nlittle if any breeziness today, but with 20-25 mph afternoon and\nearly evening gusts on Friday and Saturday. Chances for isolated\nshowers and thunderstorms are likely to remain possible across\nthe eastern districts through at least early next week while\ntemperatures eventually fall back into a normal range.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530-532-533.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST this evening for AZZ531-\n     535>544-546-548>551-559.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-566-567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
